WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
[Sound of ambulance doors being  opened.]

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.900
[Paramedic:] Ready?

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.540
[Clanking of wheels as gurney is removed from rear of ambulance.]

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.832
[Dave Gilbert:] This little girl is suffering from one of the true medical emergencies, status epilepticus.

00:00:15.832 --> 00:00:17.532
[Doctor:] Hello. [Paramedic:] Hi Doc.

00:00:17.532 --> 00:00:24.665
[Paramedic:] Hi. We've got a 13-year-old girl here that has a history of seizure disorder since she was 10 months old.

00:00:24.665 --> 00:00:32.731
She takes Dilantin and phenobarb, and for some reason, she didn't take her medicine today and went into generalized status...

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:39.180
[Dave Gilbert:] Fortunately, status is comparatively rare, but if you're a physician, especially if you're in family practice,

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:44.098
chances are you will run into epilepsy many hundreds of times in the course of your career.

00:00:44.098 --> 00:00:46.898
Who gets epilepsy?

00:00:46.898 --> 00:00:52.198
[Dr. Richard D. Walker:] It's not really a function of sex.

00:00:52.198 --> 00:00:57.998
It can be, occur just as readily in males as in females.

00:00:57.998 --> 00:01:00.531
It's not necessarily a function of age.

00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:04.898
It can occur very early in life or very late in life.

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:10.864
It's not necessarily a function of social-economic position in life.

00:01:10.865 --> 00:01:12.631
Who gets it, it can be anybody.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:14.265
[Dave Gilbert:] It can be anybody.

00:01:14.265 --> 00:01:19.931
In fact, there may be as many as four to six million persons with epilepsy in the United States.

00:01:19.932 --> 00:01:26.032
To help you keep abreast of the latest developments in the diagnosis and treatment of this important disorder, we have assembled

00:01:26.032 --> 00:01:32.698
some of the most distinguished epileptologists in the world as Doctors Talk about Epilepsy.

00:01:32.698 --> 00:01:39.964
[Dr. David D. Daly:] The essence of epilepsy is the occasional excessive discharge of nerve cells in the brain.

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:45.965
Whenever we see a patient for the first time, we ask ourselves two questions.

00:01:45.965 --> 00:01:55.165
First, do the nerve cells in the brain discharge only in a single area, creating a focal seizure,

00:01:55.165 --> 00:02:03.231
or do the nerve cells in the brain discharge at once over the entire brain, creating a generalized seizure?

00:02:03.232 --> 00:02:12.865
This is important to decide, because it helps us in seeking the underlying cause and also in the selection of the appropriate medication.

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:19.965
The second question that we ask is, and this is already partly answered by having decided the type of seizure,

00:02:19.965 --> 00:02:24.565
is what is the underlying disease in the brain causing the seizures?

00:02:24.565 --> 00:02:26.031
There are many diseases.

00:02:26.032 --> 00:02:28.698
Some of these are related to the age of the patient.

00:02:28.698 --> 00:02:36.598
And therefore, the variety of investigations undertaken may be only a few, or it may be many.

00:02:36.598 --> 00:02:47.731
When we talk about focal or partial seizures, we refer to seizures which may arise, for example, in the primary motor cortex,

00:02:47.732 --> 00:02:51.865
or they may arise in the temporal lobe of the brain.

00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:55.065
The type of seizure in each of these is quite different.

00:02:55.065 --> 00:03:01.565
[Dave Gilbert:] Here, the various types of seizures are grouped using the international classification of epileptic seizures,

00:03:01.565 --> 00:03:06.465
which is based on the site of origin, as determined clinically and by the EEG.

00:03:06.465 --> 00:03:11.240
They include partial seizures; that is, seizures which begin locally,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.898
and general seizures, those which are bilaterally symmetrical and without any local onset.

00:03:16.898 --> 00:03:21.498
Of the partial seizures, those in which there is no loss of consciousness are called simple.

00:03:21.498 --> 00:03:30.664
These include motor seizures, like the Jacksonian, as well as sensory, autonomic, and compound forms.

00:03:30.665 --> 00:03:35.765
The complex-partial seizures, those in which there is an impairment of consciousness, take several forms.

00:03:35.765 --> 00:03:40.198
Impairment of consciousness only, and those with cognitive symptomology,

00:03:40.198 --> 00:03:45.598
affective symptomology, psychosensory symptomology, psychomotor symptomology,

00:03:45.598 --> 00:03:47.698
and compound forms.

00:03:47.698 --> 00:03:55.998
The generalized seizures include the familiar "absence" or "petit mal," bilateral myoclonus, infantile spasms,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:07.600
clonic seizures, tonic seizures, tonic-clonic or grand mal seizures, atonic seizures, and akinetic seizures.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:16.265
[Dr. David D. Daly:] The focal seizure signifies the existence of a focal disease in the brain.

00:04:16.265 --> 00:04:23.765
On the other hand, certain seizures begin without warning and may involve the entire brain at once.

00:04:23.765 --> 00:04:29.031
An example of the latter is the so-called absence or petit mal attack,

00:04:29.032 --> 00:04:34.665
in which the nerve cells discharge over the entire brain at one time.

00:04:34.665 --> 00:04:39.898
[Dr. J. Kiffin Penry:] Well, of the many interesting things about epilepsy that are important to the practicing physician,

00:04:39.898 --> 00:04:46.964
the two most important things, of course, are diagnosis and treatment, and diagnosis, in itself, is difficult,

00:04:46.965 --> 00:04:53.331
because many patients go for a period of one to two years before they reach a definitive diagnosis

00:04:53.332 --> 00:04:56.132
so that definitive treatment can begin.

00:04:56.132 --> 00:04:59.732
[Dr. Robert S. Dow:] It may be a tumor. It might be trauma.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:09.265
It might be any of the diseases that affect the brain, can have convulsions or epilepsy as one of their manifestations,

00:05:09.265 --> 00:05:20.065
but when it starts in childhood, oftentimes, there are no gross brain abnormalities other than the hyperirritability of the brain,

00:05:20.065 --> 00:05:22.565
which is the characteristic feature of epilepsy.

00:05:22.565 --> 00:05:28.098
[Dr. J. Kiffin Penry:] The difficulty is that the doctor should recognize there are many other minor forms of epilepsy,

00:05:28.098 --> 00:05:38.564
and he should be aware of the broad number of manifestations that may appear, anything from a very brief, blank stare,

00:05:38.565 --> 00:05:45.098
such as that, or hesitation in speech, to many symptoms the patient may complain of.

00:05:45.098 --> 00:05:55.398
But once he has an index of suspicion that the patient could have epilepsy, that he thinks of this, then a definitive workup is necessary.

00:05:55.398 --> 00:05:58.764
[Dave Gilbert:] And so the diagnostic process begins.

00:05:58.765 --> 00:06:08.131
[Dr. Fritz Dreifuss:] The only reliable way of making a diagnosis of epilepsy is by the history that the patient gives

00:06:08.132 --> 00:06:16.798
of the events which occur in relation to an attack, and the history, which is given in corroboration

00:06:16.798 --> 00:06:20.198
by those who have seen the seizures occurring.

00:06:20.198 --> 00:06:24.464
[Dr. Robert S. Dow:] This usually requires the cooperation of the family or some member

00:06:24.465 --> 00:06:29.298
of the community who has seen the patient in a convulsion, or whatever the patient is having,

00:06:29.298 --> 00:06:35.398
and is able to give you a very detailed description of exactly the course of events throughout the time

00:06:35.398 --> 00:06:39.398
the patient goes into the seizure and loses consciousness and recovers.

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:47.464
This is, by far, the most important part of the examination and the identification of the patient with epilepsy.

00:06:47.464 --> 00:06:56.598
Then we go into the past history of possible things that could have happened to that patient that would be the, uh, as a possible cause.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.998
We take into consideration the age of the patient. All of these factors are taken into the history.

00:07:02.998 --> 00:07:08.964
Then a physical examination and a neurological examination is conducted.

00:07:08.965 --> 00:07:14.898
You check the function of muscles, the sensation of different parts of the body.

00:07:14.898 --> 00:07:16.764
You check the deep tendon reflexes.

00:07:16.765 --> 00:07:22.798
You check the patient's gait and coordination and all of these features, and it's done in a systematic way,

00:07:22.798 --> 00:07:28.264
so that you get a survey of all of the function of all the parts of the nervous system.

00:07:28.265 --> 00:07:36.398
[Dr. J. Kiffin Penry:] And of course, if abnormalities are found there, then these must be pursued also with appropriate

00:07:36.398 --> 00:07:46.964
diagnostic methods, such as examination of the head with the skull film, a lumbar spinal puncture, and electroencephalogram.

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:54.131
Depending upon what these show, of course, the patient then has to be referred to a specialist.

00:07:54.132 --> 00:08:03.332
But let us go on and assume that he's been able, with his own workup and by requesting these studies,

00:08:03.332 --> 00:08:09.665
or with the help of a consultation by a neurologist or some other specialist who is interested in epilepsy,

00:08:09.665 --> 00:08:12.398
to make a definitive diagnosis.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:19.700
The next most important area is to realize that now we have already at least 13 marketed anti-epileptic drugs,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.732
and that there are two more about to become available widely for use in practice.

00:08:24.732 --> 00:08:32.565
And now you can see the importance of the diagnosis, because these drugs should be prescribed on the basis

00:08:32.565 --> 00:08:35.798
of which type of seizure the patient has been having.

00:08:35.798 --> 00:08:43.131
For the grand mal seizure, the most commonly used medication, of course, is phenobarbital and diphenylhydantoin.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:51.920
For the complex-partial seizure, which has been called psychomotor or temporal-lobe type seizures,

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:59.132
if the phenobarbital and diphenylhydantoin have not been helpful here, primidone has been helpful in some of these patients.

00:08:59.132 --> 00:09:05.165
So you can see, then, that the definitive diagnosis becomes very important for the practicing doctor

00:09:05.165 --> 00:09:07.798
to decide which drug to prescribe.

00:09:07.798 --> 00:09:12.164
He may get this information with his consultation, or he may be skilled enough

00:09:12.165 --> 00:09:16.865
and interested enough in treating epilepsy, of course, to know about these medications.

00:09:16.865 --> 00:09:19.198
[Dave Gilbert:] Here are the most frequently used drugs.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:28.860
For partial simple seizures: diphenylhydantoin, or Dilantin, phenobarbital, or Luminal, and primidone, or Mysoline.

00:09:28.865 --> 00:09:37.665
For partial-complex seizures: diphenylhydantoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, or Tegretol, and primidone.

00:09:37.665 --> 00:09:45.398
For partial-complex seizures, which have become secondarily generalized: tonic-clonic convulsions, diphenylhydantoin,

00:09:45.398 --> 00:09:49.698
phenobarbital, carbamazepine, and primidone.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:56.998
Automatism, diphenylhydantoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, and primidone.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:07.260
For generalized seizures: tonic-clonic convulsions, diphenylhydantoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, and primidone.

00:10:07.265 --> 00:10:13.965
For absence, or petit mal: ethosuximide, or Zarontin, and clonazepam.

00:10:13.965 --> 00:10:18.398
For myoclonus: ethosuximide and clonazepam.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:25.232
[Dr. Donald Tower:] Any patient with epilepsy has to look forward to the possibility of taking drugs all his life,

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:36.240
and this creates a special problem in drug therapy, because the long-term effects of drugs are really not very well understood.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:41.060
There are not very many areas in medical therapy,

00:10:41.065 --> 00:10:47.631
where therapy has to be carried on as long as in epilepsy and some of the other neurological disorders.

00:10:47.632 --> 00:10:51.432
[Dr. Fritz Dreifuss:] Each one seems to have its own particular side effects.

00:10:51.432 --> 00:11:01.798
Diphenylhydantoin, or Dilantin, as it's commonly known, produces unsteadiness, or ataxia, in large overdose.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:08.132
Prolonged administration of diphenylhydantoin may affect the gums.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:15.360
It may possibly affect blood-forming organs. It may cause skin rash.

00:11:15.365 --> 00:11:17.931
It may cause excessive hair growth.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:24.420
It may have effects on folic acid metabolism, or vitamin D metabolism.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:32.332
These are relatively rare side effects but are things that we, who administer these drugs, very frequently,

00:11:32.332 --> 00:11:37.565
do have to be on mind as we follow patients over long periods of time.

00:11:37.565 --> 00:11:45.765
Phenobarbital, apart from its soporific effects, appears to have an exciting effect on children,

00:11:45.765 --> 00:11:50.198
particularly children who already have a tendency to hyperactivity.

00:11:50.198 --> 00:11:57.098
Primidone, or Mysoline, as it's commonly known, occasionally has effects on the patient's personality.

00:11:57.098 --> 00:12:01.564
Some children have personality changes for the worse while on that medication.

00:12:01.565 --> 00:12:07.998
Carbamazepine, or Tegretol, is a drug which has been recently introduced for the treatment of complex partial seizures.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:18.032
Its main side effects are those of bone marrow depletion, and this requires frequent blood counts to try to avoid the development thereof.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:21.660
Liver function studies are also frequently monitored.

00:12:21.665 --> 00:12:26.198
[Dr. David D. Daly:] Like all drugs, anti-epileptic drugs are a two-edged sword.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:31.460
In addition to their therapeutic effect, they may have toxic effects.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:41.798
We classify toxicity into three types: dose-related, idiosyncratic, and long-term toxicity.

00:12:41.798 --> 00:12:50.764
By dose-related, we mean symptoms of toxicity that are specifically related to the amount of medication that the patient takes.

00:12:50.765 --> 00:12:57.931
Most of the dose-related toxicities involve the central nervous system and can be readily detected

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:05.232
by measuring the serum levels of the drugs by a technique such as gas-liquid chromatography or GLC.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:09.732
Dose-related toxicities may appear at any time.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:19.532
In contrast, idiosyncratic toxicities are peculiar to the patient, are unpredictable, and may appear

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:23.365
within a very short time after the initiation of the drug.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:33.232
These cannot be detected by measuring serum levels and usually involve organs outside the central nervous system;

00:13:33.232 --> 00:13:36.032
for example, the bone marrow or the skin.

00:13:36.032 --> 00:13:41.465
[Dr. Donald Tower:] There can be a buildup of effects due to other changes in the patient's environment

00:13:41.465 --> 00:13:44.631
or any current infections or other diseases.

00:13:44.632 --> 00:13:53.398
The toxicity of the drug may suddenly become manifest, even though the patient has gone along quite well for many years before that.

00:13:53.398 --> 00:14:00.964
This is a constant problem. In addition to that, we have had, in the past,

00:14:00.965 --> 00:14:07.931
very little knowledge of exactly how individuals handled the various drugs that they were taking.

00:14:07.932 --> 00:14:15.298
I'm talking about how well they absorb them, how well they maintain a blood level, how rapidly they dispose of them.

00:14:15.298 --> 00:14:22.164
All these things that are related to therapy, and it's only in the last decade, and in fact,

00:14:22.165 --> 00:14:29.465
more properly in the last five years, that we've had techniques readily available, so that we can measure the blood levels,

00:14:29.465 --> 00:14:36.065
not only of the drugs themselves, but of the major forms in which they're metabolized by the body,

00:14:36.065 --> 00:14:43.720
so that we can have a good guide to the relationship between dose, blood level, and therapeutic response.

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:48.660
[Dave Gilbert:] One relatively new technique is gas-liquid chromatography.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:51.398
[Dr. James MacDonald Watson:] Do you know why this is being done?

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:53.660
Remember those pills you're taking?

00:14:53.665 --> 00:14:59.231
We're going to find out whether the pills are in the blood, how much of the medicine is in you.

00:14:59.232 --> 00:15:02.432
[Doctor:] You can tell if a patient is taking the drugs.

00:15:02.432 --> 00:15:08.732
You can tell if the patient is reacting properly to the drug or the way you would expect them to react to the drug.

00:15:08.732 --> 00:15:13.832
You can tell if a patient is getting too much drug, if the patient's becoming toxic on a particular drug,

00:15:13.832 --> 00:15:20.865
and you can differentiate between which drug is toxic and which drugs are being effective in the treatment of epilepsy.

00:15:20.865 --> 00:15:26.865
[Dr. James MacDonald Watson:] Patients come to the laboratory here and submit the blood sample directly, or at least,

00:15:26.865 --> 00:15:35.065
in this laboratory, we have the availability of services by mail, so to speak, in which a similar specimen might arrive

00:15:35.065 --> 00:15:40.898
from a physician somewhere, who has already been supplied with a preprinted mailer,

00:15:40.898 --> 00:15:44.998
and the blood specimen arrives in a small tube, having already been reduced to serum.

00:15:44.998 --> 00:15:51.631
[Dave Gilbert:] Doctors can obtain information about gas-liquid chromatography services by contacting the Epilepsy Foundation of America,

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:56.165
Washington D.C., or through the foundation chapter in their area.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:02.160
For those who can't be helped by drugs, surgery is sometimes a possibility.

00:16:02.165 --> 00:16:05.165
For this patient, there was no other choice.

00:16:05.165 --> 00:16:10.360
[Woman:] Oh, I was, I couldn't bear to continue with the life I was living.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:15.060
I was scared to death. I was depressed. I was unhappy.

00:16:15.065 --> 00:16:17.831
I was actually quite suicidal, too.

00:16:17.832 --> 00:16:22.665
[Dr. Theodore Rasmussen:] With certain limitations, no brain is better than bad brain.

00:16:22.665 --> 00:16:32.300
For example, a patient whose frontal lobe is very epileptogenic, it's generating high-amplitude seizure discharges

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:38.398
that spread throughout the rest of the brain.

00:16:38.398 --> 00:16:43.631
These sometimes interfere with the normal function of the rest of the brain,

00:16:43.632 --> 00:16:51.398
and that removal of this frontal lobe, even though you are taking away a large area of the frontal lobe,

00:16:51.400 --> 00:17:00.340
if it results in eradication of these big seizure discharges, the rest of the brain

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:08.798
can function more effectively than it could when it was being bombarded by the epileptic discharges.

00:17:08.798 --> 00:17:13.698
[Woman:] I had a portion of the medial portion of the right temporal lobe removed.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:19.480
It was decided that the focus was located in the hippocampal gyrus.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:24.720
So a small section of that was taken out. I believe it was two centimeters.

00:17:24.732 --> 00:17:29.632
[Dave Gilbert:] Surgery is an increasingly important weapon in the medical arsenal against epilepsy.

00:17:29.632 --> 00:17:31.465
[Woman:] I'm studying much better.

00:17:31.465 --> 00:17:35.431
[Dave Gilbert:] Still, there seems little question that drugs will remain the major method of treatment.

00:17:35.432 --> 00:17:39.565
But there may soon be other ways of fighting epilepsy.

00:17:39.565 --> 00:17:43.498
[Dr. David D. Daly:] There are always questions about what new advances are on the horizon.

00:17:43.498 --> 00:17:48.864
One, of course, is the continued development of new anti-epileptic drugs.

00:17:48.865 --> 00:17:57.698
We do have clues to the development of new drugs, which are unrelated chemically to any of the existing drugs.

00:17:57.698 --> 00:18:05.664
Another surgical treatment, which has been proposed recently, differs from the prior types of surgical treatment,

00:18:05.665 --> 00:18:09.731
which consisted of attempting to remove the epileptic focus.

00:18:09.732 --> 00:18:14.998
Stimulation of the cerebellum has been proposed as such a technique.

00:18:14.998 --> 00:18:22.264
Another technique has been referred to as biofeedback and would be more properly described

00:18:22.265 --> 00:18:28.798
as a form of operant conditioning, in which the patient's own brain waves are used to signal him

00:18:28.798 --> 00:18:36.198
as to whether his brain cells are acting in an appropriate fashion.

00:18:36.198 --> 00:18:42.131
Both of these are highly experimental. Their usefulness remains to be established.

00:18:42.132 --> 00:18:47.598
[Clicking, static sound]

00:18:47.598 --> 00:18:54.764
[Dr. Arthur Ward:] In the monkeys, the activity of single cells on the surface of the brain can be recorded, and the monkeys can be trained

00:18:54.765 --> 00:19:01.531
in such a fashion that they can, in ways that are not entirely clear, change the rate of firing of these cells.

00:19:01.532 --> 00:19:03.732
We know what nerve cells do is send out signals.

00:19:03.732 --> 00:19:07.132
These signals can be recorded electrically with a fine electrode.

00:19:07.132 --> 00:19:12.232
The monkey is told how fast his cells are discharging by a meter, which is placed in front of him.

00:19:12.232 --> 00:19:19.232
He knows when the meter goes to the right, that the cell discharge is going up, and when it goes to the left, the cell discharge is decreasing.

00:19:19.232 --> 00:19:25.132
On the basis of this training, he has learned that when a given light goes on, he will be rewarded if he

00:19:25.132 --> 00:19:27.632
can increase the rate of discharge of that cell.

00:19:27.632 --> 00:19:32.565
Thus, when the meter goes to the right, he now, and when it goes all the way to the right, he gets this little reward of applesauce,

00:19:32.565 --> 00:19:38.965
and goes back to work and gets the cell firing fast again and is rewarded with applesauce every few seconds.

00:19:38.965 --> 00:19:44.065
How a monkey can, how with the 10 billion cells he has in his brain, increase the rate of firing

00:19:44.065 --> 00:19:47.798
of a single cell of this kind, is obviously an amazing phenomenon.

00:19:47.798 --> 00:19:57.431
Whether the human can do this, we don't know, but we may be able to train both monkeys and, subsequently, if that is the case, man,

00:19:57.432 --> 00:20:02.332
to control signals in such a fashion so that the number of times the seizures are precipitated

00:20:02.332 --> 00:20:07.032
will be decreased by conditioning associated with this, if you want to call it biofeedback.

00:20:07.032 --> 00:20:13.432
There's some evidence in the monkeys, that the monkeys who have been in these conditioning experiments, which you've seen,

00:20:13.432 --> 00:20:17.432
epileptic monkeys who have been in these conditioning experiments,

00:20:17.432 --> 00:20:20.498
although no attempt was made in the conditioning to actually suppress their seizures,

00:20:20.498 --> 00:20:26.064
nevertheless, just the act of trying to modify the activity of single cells in the area of irritation

00:20:26.065 --> 00:20:31.731
has resulted, in several of the animals, in a very dramatic decrease in the number of seizures.

00:20:31.732 --> 00:20:34.098
One of them, in fact, has become seizure-free.

00:20:34.098 --> 00:20:41.531
[Dr. David D. Daly:] Another aspect of what is ahead is can we prevent the epileptic process from occurring at all?

00:20:41.532 --> 00:20:49.165
There are reasons to think that we may prevent the development of seizures, for example, in the person who has suffered

00:20:49.165 --> 00:20:54.598
a head injury, by administering antiepileptic drugs immediately after the head injury,

00:20:54.598 --> 00:20:57.331
whether or not seizures have occurred.

00:20:57.332 --> 00:21:00.865
Other areas of prevention also may hold promise.

00:21:00.865 --> 00:21:04.131
[Dr. J. Preston Robb:] I think the outlook is very hopeful.

00:21:04.132 --> 00:21:15.732
Indeed, in the last 10 years, we've seen real advances, and the patients lives have improved a great deal, in every area.

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:21.620
[Dr. David D. Daly:] To sum this up, and to pull together the thoughts of all of us, the approach to

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:25.932
every patient with epilepsy should be a logical one.

00:21:25.932 --> 00:21:30.565
Description of the type of seizures, a search for the underlying cause,

00:21:30.565 --> 00:21:36.331
a decision on which drugs to use, based on a knowledge of the types of seizures,

00:21:36.332 --> 00:21:42.665
assurance that adequate amounts of drug are used, particularly by measuring blood levels

00:21:42.665 --> 00:21:51.298
of the antiepileptic drugs, and a willingness to be patient, to strive for complete seizure control,

00:21:51.298 --> 00:21:55.231
knowing that this takes time and is often difficult.

00:21:55.232 --> 00:22:04.165
And finally, an understanding that these problems that beset the patient may lead to other disturbances in adjustment,

00:22:04.165 --> 00:22:10.465
and in the patient's way of life, and a willingness of the physician to understand and help resolve these.

00:22:10.465 --> 00:22:17.431
[Dr. Richard D. Walker:] Epilepsy is a very difficult thing to treat, in that there are many drugs, and they require adjusting,

00:22:17.432 --> 00:22:27.498
and they require adjusting regularly, and most physicians are not prepared to do this.

00:22:27.498 --> 00:22:35.231
And very often, what happens is the patient starts on a drug, and it doesn't work,

00:22:35.232 --> 00:22:39.565
so they throw up their hands and say, look, it's no good.

00:22:39.565 --> 00:22:46.131
Now whenever a patient leaves here, I always tell them or their relatives, look,

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:50.665
the medicine we're giving you now may be no good.

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:55.132
If it isn't any good, don't chuck it.

00:22:55.132 --> 00:22:56.565
Get on the phone.

00:22:56.565 --> 00:22:59.565
Telephone me and tell me it isn't any good.

00:22:59.565 --> 00:23:01.365
You won't hurt my feelings.

00:23:01.365 --> 00:23:04.365
Tell me it isn't any good and we'll change it.

00:23:04.365 --> 00:23:14.065
And in this way, we work together with the parents or the patient and work out a regime that is effective.

00:23:14.065 --> 00:23:23.998
I think the point I would like to make most strongly is that patients with recurring epileptic seizures can be helped.

00:23:23.998 --> 00:23:29.764
It requires hard work, in many instances, but they can be helped.

00:23:29.765 --> 00:23:39.098
[Dave Gilbert:] They can be helped, and chances are, Doctor, that their most important source of help will be you.

00:23:39.098 --> 00:23:44.264
This has been a professional education service of the Epilepsy Foundation of America,

00:23:44.264 --> 00:23:50.720
whose primary mission is to interpret the needs of the person with epilepsy to the society in which he lives.

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:30.100
[End of film]

